answer.text:
I’m identifying the transistor terminals and feedback polarity first, since the figure has no component values or stated modeling assumptions. The result will therefore be symbolic, with the usual small-signal MOS assumptions made explicit.
Assume `M1` is an NMOS in saturation, `VDD` is AC ground, gate current is negligible, and body effect is ignored. Define

\[
\beta=\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}, \qquad
R_P=R_D\parallel(R_1+R_2), \qquad
g_o=\frac{1}{r_o}.
\]

The feedback divider gives

\[
v_f=\beta v_o.
\]

Since the input is applied at the source,

\[
v_{gs}=v_f-v_i.
\]

An increase in \(v_i\) reduces \(v_{gs}\), reducing drain current and raising \(v_o\). The resulting increase in \(v_f\) restores \(v_{gs}\), so the circuit uses negative voltage-series feedback.

Using drain-node KCL,

\[
\left(\frac{1}{R_D}+\frac{1}{R_1+R_2}+g_o+g_m\beta\right)v_o
=(g_m+g_o)v_i.
\]

Thus the small-signal voltage gain is

\[
\boxed{
\frac{v_o}{v_i}
=
\frac{(g_m+g_o)R_P}
{1+(g_o+g_m\beta)R_P}
}
\]

or, if \(r_o\) is neglected,

\[
\boxed{
A_v=\frac{g_mR_P}{1+g_m\beta R_P}
}
\]

If the divider loading is negligible compared with \(R_D\), use \(R_P\simeq R_D\):

\[
\boxed{
A_v\simeq
\frac{g_mR_D}
{1+g_mR_D\left(\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}\right)}
}
\]

For a large loop gain, \(g_m\beta R_P\gg1\),

\[
\boxed{
A_v\simeq\frac{1}{\beta}
=1+\frac{R_1}{R_2}
}
\]

so the closed-loop gain is mainly set by the resistor ratio.

The feedback also changes the port resistances. With an ideal voltage source driving \(v_i\),

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}=
\frac{1+(g_o+g_m\beta)R_P}{g_m+g_o}
}
\]

and, for \(r_o\to\infty\),

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}\simeq \frac{1}{g_m}+\beta R_P
}
\]

The output resistance, with the input source AC-grounded, is

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}=
\frac{R_P}{1+(g_o+g_m\beta)R_P}
}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}\simeq
\frac{R_P}{1+g_m\beta R_P}
}
\]

when \(r_o\) is ignored. Thus, the negative feedback raises the input resistance and lowers the output resistance.

For DC bias, the divider still sets

\[
V_F=\beta V_O,
\]

while the drain-node equation is

\[
\frac{V_{DD}-V_O}{R_D}
=
I_D+\frac{V_O}{R_1+R_2}.
\]

Together with the MOS saturation current relation using \(V_{GS}=V_F-V_{\text{in,DC}}\), this determines the operating point.

With \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) treated as ac ground, apply a test voltage \(v_t\) at the output. Let the small-signal voltage at node \(X\) be \(v_x\).

Ignoring body effect, node \(X\) gives

\[
(g_{m1}+g_{o1}+g_{o2})v_x-g_{o1}v_t=0 .
\]

Thus,

\[
\frac{i_t}{v_t}
=
\frac{g_{o1}g_{o2}}
{g_{m1}+g_{o1}+g_{o2}}.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=
r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

or equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=
r_{o1}+(1+g_{m1}r_{o1})r_{o2}
}
\]

For \(g_{m1}r_{o1}\gg 1\),

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}.}
\]

If body effect of \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\).
